"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"][5]={"school_computer_science_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Tables, keys and relationships<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Databases and data models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4 – From a measured signal to a\ntable<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain entities, attributes,\nkeys and cardinalities in a lending model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which copy is out on loan?<\/h2>\n<p>The library has two copies of the same book. One is on the shelf and\nthe other is on loan. If a table refers to both copies by their title\nalone, the library cannot assign a return to a particular copy. The data\nmodel needs separate identifiers for this task.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Entities, attributes and keys<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>entity<\/strong> is something we treat as an individual\nobject in the model, such as a specific copy. An <strong>entity\ntype<\/strong> groups objects described in the same way. An\n<strong>attribute<\/strong> is a feature such as the title or the page\ncount. In a table model, an entity type often corresponds to a table, an\nentity to a row and an attribute to a column.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>primary key<\/strong> uniquely identifies a row. It can\nconsist of one or more columns. Several copies may have the same title,\nso an assigned copy ID is more suitable. A <strong>foreign key<\/strong>\nrefers to a uniquely identifiable row in a table, often through that\nrow’s primary key.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cardinality<\/strong> describes how many entities on one side\nof a relationship can be linked to an entity on the other. A member can\nborrow many copies on different dates. The same copy can be borrowed by\nseveral members over time. The direct relationship across the whole\nhistory is therefore <strong>many-to-many<\/strong> (n:m). A separate\nloan row represents each individual event.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_018_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A member and a book copy each have zero to many loans. Each loan belongs to exactly one member and one copy.\" />\n<figcaption>The loan table represents the many possible associations\nbetween members and copies as individual events.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An example with a loan history<\/h2>\n<p>We model each physical copy in its own book row. The example\nidentifiers have no meaning outside the model.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Member ID<\/th>\n<th>Display name<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>Kim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>Noor<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Book ID<\/th>\n<th>Title<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Moss<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>Orbit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Signals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Atlas<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Loan ID<\/th>\n<th>Member ID<\/th>\n<th>Book ID<\/th>\n<th>Date<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>2026-03-03<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>2026-03-04<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>2026-04-02<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For loan 10, we use member ID 7 to find the display name Kim and\nbook ID 1 to find the title Moss. The key of the loan is 10.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Loans 10 and 11 both belong to Kim. A member can have several loan\nrows.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Loans 10 and 12 both concern the same copy of Moss on different\ndates. A table that allows one book per member could not represent the\nfull history.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Every loan points to exactly one member and one copy. In the other\ndirection, a member or a copy can be associated with zero, one or\nseveral loan rows.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The library adds another row with the title <code>Orbit<\/code>. May\nits book ID be 2? Which values may be shared?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the pair of member ID and book ID alone unsuitable as the\nloan’s primary key for this history?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Book ID 2 is already in use in the example. The additional copy gets\na new book ID. The title may be the same.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A member may borrow the same copy on different dates. The pair would\nthen recur. A separate loan ID distinguishes the individual events.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The book row in this example identifies a physical copy, including a\ntitle that may recur. A larger library might place bibliographic data in\na separate edition table. Key references check whether the book and\nmember exist. By themselves, they cannot ensure that two loan periods\nfor the same copy do not overlap. That requires additional rules and\nreturn dates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A primary key identifies a row, and a foreign key links to a row. A\nseparate relationship table supports repeated events and can store\ndetails such as the date of each event.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2 – SQL queries: filtering and\nsorting<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.1 · Explain entities,\nattributes, keys and cardinalities in a lending model. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/Rahmenlehrplanprojekt/amtliche_Fassung/Teil_C_Informatik_2015_11_10.pdf\">Bildungsserver\nBerlin-Brandenburg · Senatsverwaltung Berlin und MBJS Brandenburg –\nRahmenlehrplan Teil C – Informatik, Wahlpflichtfach, Jahrgangsstufen\n7–10<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.7 · Explain entities, attributes, keys\nand cardinalities in a lending model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/10/informatik\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 10 – Informatik<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Inf10 LB1\n· Explain entities, attributes, keys and cardinalities in a lending\nmodel. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – SQL queries: filtering and sorting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Databases and data models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Lower secondary (core)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1 – Tables, keys and\nrelationships<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Run SQLite queries using SELECT,\nWHERE and ORDER BY.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Finding the right books in a large collection<\/h2>\n<p>A reading group wants science books of at least 100 pages, with the\nlongest books first. The library records the title, shelf and page count\nfor each copy. Which SQL query returns exactly the list it needs?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What the parts of SELECT mean<\/h2>\n<p><strong>SQL<\/strong> is a language used, among other things, to query\nrelational databases. A query describes which rows and columns should be\nin the result. The database system chooses the steps needed to compute\nthat result.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>FROM<\/code> specifies the source table.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>WHERE<\/code> tests a condition for each row. The row is kept\nif the condition is true.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>SELECT<\/code> lists the columns or expressions to show in the\nresult. This selection is called <strong>projection<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>ORDER BY<\/code> controls the order of the result rows.\n<code>ASC<\/code> sorts in ascending order and <code>DESC<\/code> in\ndescending order. Additional sort columns resolve ties from left to\nright.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The query is written with <code>SELECT<\/code> first. When explaining\nits result it is often easier to start with <code>FROM<\/code> and\n<code>WHERE<\/code>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_019_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"The query starts logically with the book table, filters by shelf and page count, projects title and pages, then orders by page count and book ID.\" />\n<figcaption>The diagram follows the meaning of the SQL clauses. The\ndatabase engine can execute the query differently.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking rows on paper and querying in SQLite<\/h2>\n<p>Create the four sample records in a fresh SQLite database. If the\nSQLite command line is installed, start it with\n<code>sqlite3 :memory:<\/code>. Run all SQL blocks in this lesson in the\nsame session and start a new session for a new lesson. The titles, shelf\nnames and page counts are fictional. Our assumption here is that all\nbooks in the table have a positive page count.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> book (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    book_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    title TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    shelf TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    pages <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">CHECK<\/span> (pages <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> book <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Moss&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;science&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Orbit&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;fiction&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">180<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Signals&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;science&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">110<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Atlas&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;science&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>);<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Check the shelf. <code>Orbit<\/code> is on the <code>fiction<\/code>\nshelf and is excluded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>Moss<\/code>, <code>Signals<\/code> and <code>Atlas<\/code> all\nalso satisfy <code>pages &gt;= 100<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Show the titles and page counts. <code>Moss<\/code> and\n<code>Atlas<\/code> each have 120 pages, so ascending book ID breaks\ntheir tie. <code>Signals<\/code> follows with 110 pages.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> title, pages<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> book<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> shelf <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;science&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">AND<\/span> pages <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> pages <span class=\"kw\">DESC<\/span>, book_id <span class=\"kw\">ASC<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>title<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">pages<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Moss<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">120<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Atlas<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">120<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signals<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">110<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Using <code>book_id<\/code> to determine the order does not make it a\nvisible result column.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Write a query for the titles of science books with exactly 120\npages. Sort them by title in ascending order. Give the result.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Suppose <code>book_id ASC<\/code> is removed from the example query.\nWhich row is still certain to come last? What ordering information is\nmissing?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><p>The query filters on <code>shelf = 'science'<\/code> and\n<code>pages = 120<\/code>. Sorting by title gives <code>Atlas<\/code>,\nthen <code>Moss<\/code>.<\/p><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><p><code>Signals<\/code> is still last because it has 110 pages. The\nremaining sort key does not determine the order of the two rows with 120\npages.<\/p><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Query for exercise 1<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> title<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-2\"><a href=\"#cb3-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> book<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-3\"><a href=\"#cb3-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> shelf <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;science&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">AND<\/span> pages <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-4\"><a href=\"#cb3-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> title <span class=\"kw\">ASC<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>title<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Atlas<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Moss<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Without <code>ORDER BY<\/code>, the output may appear in a familiar\norder. The query does not guarantee that order. In this example, text\nvalues go in single quotes. Text ordering depends on the database’s\ncomparison rules. When writing more complex conditions, use parentheses\nto make it clear which subconditions go together.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p><code>WHERE<\/code> determines which rows remain. <code>SELECT<\/code>\ndetermines which values appear. <code>ORDER BY<\/code> with a unique\nfinal sort key gives a defined order in the sample data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3 – SQL joins, groups and\nmissing values<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/FA/IBS/user_upload/GI-Bildungsstandards_Informatik_Sek_I__2025__-_blau.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe I<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §5.1 · Run SQLite queries using\nSELECT, WHERE and ORDER BY. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html\">SQLite –\nSELECT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Simple SELECT processing · Run SQLite\nqueries using SELECT, WHERE and ORDER BY. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – SQL joins, groups and missing values<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Databases and data models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2 – SQL queries: filtering and\nsorting<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain JOIN, GROUP BY and NULL\nthrough verified queries.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counting copies with no loans as well<\/h2>\n<p>The library wants to know how many times each copy has been borrowed.\nSome copies have no entries in the loan table. Counting only there would\nmake those copies disappear from the list. How can we keep them and show\na count of zero?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joining and grouping rows<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>join<\/strong> combines rows from two tables according to a\ncondition. <code>ON book.book_id = loan.book_id<\/code> associates a book\nrow with every loan having that book ID. An <strong>INNER JOIN<\/strong>\nreturns matching pairs. A <strong>LEFT JOIN<\/strong> also keeps every\nleft-hand row without a match. In such a row, the right-hand columns are\nfilled with <code>NULL<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>In SQL, <code>NULL<\/code> represents a missing or unknown value. A\ncount of zero is the number <code>0<\/code>. The comparison\n<code>= NULL<\/code> does not return a true result. Use\n<code>IS NULL<\/code> to test whether a value is missing.<\/p>\n<p><code>GROUP BY<\/code> collects the joined rows into groups.\n<code>COUNT(*)<\/code> counts the rows in a group.\n<code>COUNT(loan.loan_id)<\/code> counts rows in the group that have a\nloan ID. The loan ID is a primary key, so a real loan row always has\none.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_020_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Book 1 appears with loan IDs 10 and 12, book 2 with 11, and books 3 and 4 each with NULL.\" />\n<figcaption>Copies with no matching loan row are kept in the\nintermediate result.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculating the number of loans for each copy<\/h2>\n<p>Run the following blocks in a fresh SQLite database. For this count,\nwe only need the book ID, title and loan ID.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>PRAGMA foreign_keys <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span>;<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> book (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    book_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    title TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> loan (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    loan_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    book_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> book(book_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> book <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Moss&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Orbit&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Signals&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Atlas&#39;<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> loan <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>);<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The join produces two rows for book 1, with loan IDs 10 and 12. It\nproduces one row for book 2, with ID 11.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Book 3 and book 4 each produce one row with a missing loan ID.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Group by book ID and title so copies with the same title stay\nseparate. Compare the two counts.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> b.book_id, b.title, <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> joined_rows,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>       <span class=\"fu\">COUNT<\/span>(l.loan_id) <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> loans<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> book <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">LEFT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> loan <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> l <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> b.book_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> l.book_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">GROUP<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> b.book_id, b.title<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-6\"><a href=\"#cb2-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> b.book_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">book_id<\/th>\n<th>title<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">joined_rows<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">loans<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td>Moss<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td>Orbit<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td>Signals<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td>Atlas<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many rows would the grouped result have with an INNER JOIN?\nWhich books would be missing?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find every book without a loan by using a LEFT JOIN and\n<code>IS NULL<\/code>. Return each book ID and title, sorted by ID.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">There would be two groups for Moss and Orbit. Signals and Atlas have\nno matching rows, so they would be absent.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">After the join, test the loan ID using <code>IS NULL<\/code>. The two\nresulting rows are books 3 and 4.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Query for exercise 2<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> b.book_id, b.title<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-2\"><a href=\"#cb3-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> book <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-3\"><a href=\"#cb3-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">LEFT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> loan <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> l <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> b.book_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> l.book_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-4\"><a href=\"#cb3-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> l.loan_id <span class=\"kw\">IS<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-5\"><a href=\"#cb3-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> b.book_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">book_id<\/th>\n<th>title<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td>Signals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td>Atlas<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A <code>WHERE<\/code> filter on a right-hand column may exclude the\nrows filled in by the LEFT JOIN. For example,\n<code>WHERE l.loan_id &gt;= 12<\/code> drops the rows with missing\nvalues. To join only loans with IDs of at least 12 while also keeping\nall books, put the extra condition in <code>ON<\/code>. Every further\njoin can multiply rows. Before counting, check what a row in the\nintermediate result represents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A LEFT JOIN preserves left-hand rows without a match. Group by the\nunit you want to count for, which is the copy here. Count a column\nguaranteed to exist in real right-hand rows when you want to count\ngenuine matches.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4 – Normalisation and data\nintegrity<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.1 · Explain JOIN, GROUP BY\nand NULL through verified queries. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_select.html\">SQLite –\nSELECT<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: FROM clause processing · Explain JOIN,\nGROUP BY and NULL through verified queries. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22\n· Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M5-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M5-L4 – Normalisation and data integrity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Databases and data models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3 – SQL joins, groups and missing\nvalues<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse redundancy and\nfunctional dependencies and justify a key-preserving decomposition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The same room name in many rows<\/h2>\n<p>A project week has a list of registrations. Each row records a\nperson’s name, the workshop and the room name. When “Studio” is renamed\n“Atelier”, a helper updates only two of the three rows. How can we\ndesign a data model in which this kind of inconsistency cannot\narise?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What depends on which key<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>functional dependency<\/strong> <code>X → Y<\/code> holds\nwhen rows with the same values for <code>X<\/code> must always have the\nsame values for <code>Y<\/code>. It follows from the domain rules for all\nvalid data, rather than an incidental pattern in a sample. A\n<strong>candidate key<\/strong> is a minimal set of columns that uniquely\nidentifies a row. It is minimal if you cannot drop any of its\ncolumns.<\/p>\n<p>Assume that a person can register only once per workshop. Each\nworkshop has exactly one room, and each room ID has exactly one name.\nColumns contain single values here. The sole candidate key of the large\ntable is the pair <code>(student_id, workshop_id)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>First normal form (1NF):<\/strong> Here each cell has one\nvalue and no list of workshops.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Second normal form (2NF):<\/strong> Every non-key attribute\nmust depend on the whole candidate key, rather than only part of it. Yet\nthe person’s name already depends on <code>student_id<\/code> and the\nworkshop details on <code>workshop_id<\/code>. We therefore move those\ndetails out of the registrations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Third normal form (3NF):<\/strong> In the remaining simple\nworkshop schema, the room name depends on <code>room_id<\/code> and\ntherefore on <code>workshop_id<\/code> indirectly. We put rooms in their\nown table.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_021_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Student ID determines the name, workshop ID determines the title and room ID, room ID determines the room name, and the student–workshop pair determines the registration date. Values then belong in student, workshop, room and registration.\" />\n<figcaption>After decomposition, each dependency belongs in the table\nwith the corresponding key.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decomposing and rejoining the tables<\/h2>\n<p>The registration keeps its two key columns and the registration date.\nWe get the name and room through joins when needed. The following\nexample runs in a fresh SQLite database.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>PRAGMA foreign_keys <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span>;<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> student (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    student_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    student_name TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> room (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    room_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    room_name TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> workshop (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    workshop_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    workshop_title TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    room_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> room(room_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> registration (<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    student_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> student(student_id),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    workshop_id <span class=\"dt\">INTEGER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">REFERENCES<\/span> workshop(workshop_id),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    registered_on TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">PRIMARY<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">KEY<\/span> (student_id, workshop_id)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> student <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Kim&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Noor&#39;<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> room <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Studio&#39;<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> workshop <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Sound&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;Light&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> registration <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;2026-03-03&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">21<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;2026-03-03&#39;<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;2026-03-04&#39;<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">UPDATE<\/span> room <span class=\"kw\">SET<\/span> room_name <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Atelier&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> room_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> s.student_name, w.workshop_title, r.room_name<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> registration <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> a<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> student <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> s <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> a.student_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> s.student_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> workshop <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> w <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> a.workshop_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> w.workshop_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> room <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> r <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> w.room_id <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> r.room_id<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-6\"><a href=\"#cb2-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> a.student_id, a.workshop_id;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>student_name<\/th>\n<th>workshop_title<\/th>\n<th>room_name<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Kim<\/td>\n<td>Sound<\/td>\n<td>Atelier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kim<\/td>\n<td>Light<\/td>\n<td>Atelier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noor<\/td>\n<td>Sound<\/td>\n<td>Atelier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Each join finds exactly one row for the relevant identifier because\nprimary keys are unique and the required foreign keys refer to existing\nentries. Selecting the remaining original columns would let you\nreconstruct every original registration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does <code>registered_on<\/code> belong to the registration\nrather than to the person or the workshop?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens in the example if Kim registers for Sound a second\ntime? What happens if someone registers for workshop 99?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A person can register for different workshops on different days.\nDifferent people can book the same workshop on different days. In this\nmodel, the pair is needed to determine the day.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Kim and Sound form the existing pair <code>(7, 20)<\/code>. The\ncomposite primary key rejects the duplicate. A registration for workshop\n99 violates the foreign key because the workshop is missing and foreign\nkey checks are enabled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions, errors and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The 3NF explanation covers this case of straightforward keys and\ndependencies. In general, for every non-trivial functional dependency\nwith one column on the right, either the left side must be a superkey or\nthe column on the right must belong to a candidate key. A superkey\nuniquely identifies a row and may contain extra columns. Changed rules,\nsuch as using several rooms for a workshop, require a revised model. A\nSQL foreign key enforces only the domain rules expressed in the\nschema.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Split the table according to dependencies supported by the domain\nrules. Store the room name with the room and the registration under the\ncomplete registration key. Primary and foreign keys make it possible to\nrejoin the information unambiguously.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L1 – Classes, objects and\ninterfaces<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/unterricht/faecher-rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplan-informatik-go-teil-c.pdf?ts=1705017673\">Senatsverwaltung\nfür Bildung, Jugend und Familie Berlin – Rahmenlehrplan für die\ngymnasiale Oberstufe – Informatik, Teil C<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2 ·\nAnalyse redundancy and functional dependencies and justify a\nkey-preserving decomposition. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/foreignkeys.html\">SQLite – SQLite\nForeign Key Support<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Foreign key support ·\nAnalyse redundancy and functional dependencies and justify a\nkey-preserving decomposition. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.rpi.edu/~sibel/csci4380/fall2026/lecture_notes/lecture5.html\">Sibel\nAdalı, Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute – CSCI 4380 Database Systems:\nLecture 5 Normalization<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Formal Definition, BCNF\nand 3NF · Analyse redundancy and functional dependencies and justify a\nkey-preserving decomposition. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
